# Delegate to cursor (experimental)

**cursor is experimental: no OS sandbox; write permission requests are approved by pactify;
results vary by model.** Run only via {{command "delegate-cursor"}}.
Read-only permission requests are denied.
Prefer the default codex harness (`/delegate` or `/delegate-codex`).

**If the user invoked this skill explicitly ({{command "delegate-cursor"}}), always delegate — whatever
the task size. The user chose the harness; do not do the work yourself instead.**

Launch command (step 2 below):

{{if eq .Host "claude"}}    pactify sub run cursor --write --worktree --wait 0s --json <<'BRIEF'
    ...complete brief from step 1...
    BRIEF
{{else}}    pactify sub run cursor --write --worktree --wait 0s --json --brief-file <brief-file>
{{end}}
Use `--read-only` for investigations and reviews. Use `--write` only with explicit permission to change files. Never raise permissions yourself.
Without `--model`, Cursor uses its own default. Preserve an explicit `--model`; models come from `agent models`.
Cursor has no separate effort setting: put it in the model id, for example `model[effort=high]`.

{{include "shared-delegation.md.tmpl"}}

Pactify runs `agent acp` (or `cursor-agent acp`) and checks that the binary is Cursor's. Write access
uses Cursor's agent mode and pactify approves its permission requests; `sandboxed=false` because
there is no OS sandbox here. Read-only access uses Cursor's ask mode, denies every permission
request, and fails the job if a before/after check finds any changed file in the repository.
Cursor's interactive questions and plan approvals are answered with a cancellation.
Default minimal config uses a private CURSOR_CONFIG_DIR, so jobs and their chats stay out of the
user's own chat history and ignore the user's Cursor settings; login is Cursor's own (keychain,
`agent login` or `CURSOR_API_KEY`). Never inspect credentials. `--with-user-config` uses the
user's Cursor settings and keeps chats in the user's history.
Quota exhaustion only appears as text and does not trigger fallback.
